WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.622
Imagine that Torrance approves a commercial project on a parcel listed in the housing-element sites inventory.

2
00:00:06.622 --> 00:00:11.137
The project is popular and economically useful, but it includes no housing.

3
00:00:11.137 --> 00:00:17.096
A commissioner says the city already completed its housing planning when the element was certified.

4
00:00:17.096 --> 00:00:26.788
Staff opens a capacity ledger and asks the better question: after this approval, do adequate sites remain for the city's unmet housing need at each income level?

5
00:00:26.788 --> 00:00:29.627
This episode is preparation for public service.

6
00:00:29.627 --> 00:00:34.639
It is not legal advice about a site, approval, demolition, or capacity calculation.

7
00:00:34.639 --> 00:00:40.256
Housing-capacity laws contain technical definitions and deadlines and are frequently amended.

8
00:00:40.256 --> 00:00:49.375
Use the current statute, current HCD guidance, the adopted housing element, permit and entitlement records, and advice from the Torrance city attorney.

9
00:00:49.375 --> 00:00:51.791
The sites inventory is a planning claim.

10
00:00:51.791 --> 00:00:57.347
It identifies parcels and assumptions showing how the jurisdiction can accommodate its RHNA.

11
00:00:57.347 --> 00:01:04.775
Those assumptions include potential units, income categories, realistic capacity, existing use, and expected redevelopment.

12
00:01:04.775 --> 00:01:06.950
Time does not freeze after adoption.

13
00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:13.774
Parcels develop, applications change density, lot lines move, uses continue, and affordability commitments shift.

14
00:01:13.774 --> 00:01:16.552
The inventory therefore needs a living ledger.

15
00:01:16.552 --> 00:01:21.685
The best-known protection is Government Code section 65863, often called no net loss.

16
00:01:21.685 --> 00:01:33.402
When a city or county approves development on an inventory site with fewer units by income category than the housing element identified, it must make specified findings about remaining capacity.

17
00:01:33.402 --> 00:01:44.755
If the remaining sites are not adequate to accommodate the unmet need, the jurisdiction generally must identify and make available sufficient replacement sites within the statutory period.

18
00:01:44.755 --> 00:01:46.809
Do not reduce this to total units.

19
00:01:46.809 --> 00:01:48.319
Income categories matter.

20
00:01:48.319 --> 00:01:55.022
Losing capacity assumed for lower-income housing cannot necessarily be cured by unused above-moderate capacity.

21
00:01:55.022 --> 00:02:03.538
The zoning and development standards on the remaining or replacement sites must meet applicable requirements for the need being accommodated.

22
00:02:03.538 --> 00:02:06.383
Build a ledger with one row per inventory site.

23
00:02:06.383 --> 00:02:21.092
Record the parcel identifier, inventory program, assumed capacity, assumed income category, zoning, realistic-capacity method, current entitlement, building permits, completed units, affordability restrictions, and remaining credited capacity.

24
00:02:21.092 --> 00:02:26.298
Add the date of the latest verification and a link to the action that changed the row.

25
00:02:26.298 --> 00:02:29.022
When an approval arrives, ask five questions.

26
00:02:29.022 --> 00:02:30.959
Was the parcel in the inventory?

27
00:02:30.959 --> 00:02:34.954
How many units and which income categories did the element assign?

28
00:02:34.954 --> 00:02:36.952
What does the approval authorize?

29
00:02:36.952 --> 00:02:40.100
How much credited capacity remains after the action?

30
00:02:40.100 --> 00:02:45.063
Does the city still have adequate sites for the remaining RHNA by income category?

31
00:02:45.063 --> 00:02:48.151
The answer can require legal and planning judgment.

32
00:02:48.151 --> 00:02:51.056
A mixed-use approval may preserve some capacity.

33
00:02:51.056 --> 00:02:56.444
A nonresidential approval may consume the development opportunity assumed in the element.

34
00:02:56.444 --> 00:02:58.018
An entitlement can expire.

35
00:02:58.018 --> 00:03:00.318
A phased project can change over time.

36
00:03:00.318 --> 00:03:03.405
Never update the ledger from an agenda title alone.

37
00:03:03.405 --> 00:03:07.158
Replacement is not just finding an empty row in a spreadsheet.

38
00:03:07.158 --> 00:03:14.664
The site must be made available with appropriate zoning and development standards within the period required by current law.

39
00:03:14.664 --> 00:03:20.657
Depending on the context, the city may need to rezone and satisfy by-right or density requirements.

40
00:03:20.657 --> 00:03:28.829
The city should not wait until the deadline to discover that environmental review, public notice, or ordinance preparation is required.

41
00:03:28.829 --> 00:03:32.037
Capacity is also protected by the Housing Crisis Act.

42
00:03:32.037 --> 00:03:42.691
Current law restricts certain actions that reduce residential development capacity, including downzoning and reductions in intensity, subject to statutory terms and exceptions.

43
00:03:42.691 --> 00:03:48.018
It also contains demolition and replacement protections and limits on housing moratoria.

44
00:03:48.018 --> 00:03:52.861
The act's provisions and relevant baseline dates have been amended and extended.

45
00:03:52.861 --> 00:03:56.372
Treat any summary as a prompt to open the current statute.

46
00:03:56.372 --> 00:03:58.549
Project review creates another risk.

47
00:03:58.549 --> 00:04:12.763
For a housing development project protected by the Housing Accountability Act, a city generally cannot deny the project or reduce its density when it complies with applicable objective standards unless demanding findings are supported.

48
00:04:12.763 --> 00:04:18.811
Conditions should not quietly remove units in a way that functions as an unlawful density reduction.

49
00:04:18.811 --> 00:04:22.198
Density bonus law can also affect the approved capacity.

50
00:04:22.198 --> 00:04:27.883
A qualifying project may receive additional density, concessions, waivers, and parking relief.

51
00:04:27.883 --> 00:04:40.766
The local ledger should record the actual approval and affordability, but staff must avoid treating bonus units or statutory relief as evidence that the underlying zoning can be reduced elsewhere without analysis.

52
00:04:40.766 --> 00:04:42.883
Demolition requires its own branch.

53
00:04:42.883 --> 00:04:50.927
Count existing protected units, occupied units, units withdrawn from rent or lease, and lower-income tenancy as current law requires.

54
00:04:50.927 --> 00:04:56.492
Identify replacement obligations, right-to-return provisions, relocation duties, and timing.

55
00:04:56.492 --> 00:05:02.722
A site that appears to add twenty units may have a smaller net effect after existing units are counted.

56
00:05:02.722 --> 00:05:06.774
Capacity accounting and tenant protection are related but distinct.

57
00:05:06.774 --> 00:05:10.766
Monitoring should connect planning, building, and finance systems.

58
00:05:10.766 --> 00:05:12.459
Planning knows entitlements.

59
00:05:12.459 --> 00:05:14.758
Building knows permits and completion.

60
00:05:14.758 --> 00:05:17.963
Housing staff know covenants and income restrictions.

61
00:05:17.963 --> 00:05:20.080
The city clerk knows final actions.

62
00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:25.040
If those records never reconcile, the APR and the capacity ledger can drift apart.

63
00:05:25.040 --> 00:05:30.907
For practical application, use a fictional inventory parcel credited with 100 lower-income units.

64
00:05:30.907 --> 00:05:34.415
Scenario A approves 80 deed-restricted lower-income units.

65
00:05:34.415 --> 00:05:36.895
Scenario B approves 80 market-rate units.

66
00:05:36.895 --> 00:05:38.830
Scenario C approves a warehouse.

67
00:05:38.830 --> 00:05:40.645
Do not assume the same result.

68
00:05:40.645 --> 00:05:52.076
Complete a worksheet for each scenario identifying the change by income category, remaining jurisdiction-wide need, possible findings, replacement question, and evidence counsel would need.

69
00:05:52.076 --> 00:05:58.861
Commissioners should ask for capacity information before acting on a relevant legislative or discretionary item.

70
00:05:58.861 --> 00:06:04.617
The staff report should identify inventory status and the no-net-loss analysis when applicable.

71
00:06:04.617 --> 00:06:08.979
If replacement may be needed, ask for the deadline and responsible body.

72
00:06:08.979 --> 00:06:14.916
If staff concludes the law does not apply, ask for the short reason and preserve it in the record.

73
00:06:14.916 --> 00:06:18.673
The commission should also receive periodic portfolio reports.

74
00:06:18.673 --> 00:06:23.035
A map is useful for orientation, but the ledger controls the arithmetic.

75
00:06:23.035 --> 00:06:32.365
Show original inventory capacity, approved capacity, permitted units, lost or changed sites, replacement actions, and remaining buffer by income category.

76
00:06:32.365 --> 00:06:35.818
A small buffer deserves attention before it reaches zero.

77
00:06:35.818 --> 00:06:37.030
Do not double count.

78
00:06:37.030 --> 00:06:42.300
One parcel should not simultaneously supply full capacity to two incompatible programs.

79
00:06:42.300 --> 00:06:45.754
Do not count an expired entitlement as completed housing.

80
00:06:45.754 --> 00:06:48.177
Do not count an application as a permit.

81
00:06:48.177 --> 00:06:54.417
Do not assume every zoned unit is realistic capacity if the adopted methodology applied a lower factor.

82
00:06:54.417 --> 00:06:56.538
The policy objective is continuity.

83
00:06:56.538 --> 00:06:59.264
The city made a planning showing at adoption.

84
00:06:59.264 --> 00:07:06.534
Every later decision should preserve an honest path from that showing to available sites, approvals, permits, and homes.

85
00:07:06.534 --> 00:07:08.776
A ledger does not force construction.

86
00:07:08.776 --> 00:07:11.745
It keeps the city's planning commitments visible.

87
00:07:11.745 --> 00:07:15.198
Details change, and statutory exceptions can be decisive.

88
00:07:15.198 --> 00:07:19.318
Current statute and city-attorney advice control every real finding.

89
00:07:19.318 --> 00:07:20.958
Now three recall questions.

90
00:07:20.958 --> 00:07:21.747
Question one.

91
00:07:21.747 --> 00:07:25.757
Why is a total-unit count insufficient for no-net-loss monitoring?

92
00:07:25.757 --> 00:07:26.182
Answer.

93
00:07:26.182 --> 00:07:32.621
The analysis tracks capacity for the remaining housing need by income category, not only a citywide total.

94
00:07:32.621 --> 00:07:33.411
Question two.

95
00:07:33.411 --> 00:07:38.149
What are the five basic questions when an inventory site receives an approval?

96
00:07:38.149 --> 00:07:38.574
Answer.

97
00:07:38.574 --> 00:07:49.326
Confirm inventory status, assigned units and income levels, the approved use and units, remaining credited capacity, and whether adequate sites still accommodate the unmet need.

98
00:07:49.326 --> 00:07:50.237
Question three.

99
00:07:50.237 --> 00:07:54.611
Does identifying replacement capacity mean merely naming another parcel?

100
00:07:54.611 --> 00:07:55.036
Answer.

101
00:07:55.036 --> 00:07:55.218
No.

102
00:07:55.218 --> 00:08:04.026
The replacement must satisfy current legal requirements and be made available with appropriate zoning and standards within the applicable period.

103
00:08:04.026 --> 00:08:09.190
Your learner artifact is a capacity ledger template and the three-scenario worksheet.

104
00:08:09.190 --> 00:08:16.783
Include fields for source document, action date, income category, remaining capacity, replacement deadline, and legal review.

105
00:08:16.783 --> 00:08:20.488
Add one reconciliation line connecting the ledger to the APR.

106
00:08:20.488 --> 00:08:36.831
This lesson draws from current California Department of Housing and Community Development 2026 fact sheets and guidance on housing elements, sites inventories, no net loss, the Housing Accountability Act, the Housing Crisis Act, replacement housing, and density bonus law.

107
00:08:36.831 --> 00:08:44.101
It also relies on current California Government Code provisions, HCD APR materials, and public Torrance planning records.

108
00:08:44.101 --> 00:08:50.109
The narration is original and copies no source prose, slide, map, recording, or proprietary dataset.

109
00:08:50.109 --> 00:08:58.040
Because details and effective dates change, confirm current statute, HCD guidance, local records, and Torrance city-attorney advice.
